From d87c47c12a42ca354f7765b99f89881894ceb999 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Illya Antonyuk <illantalex84@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 19:32:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 09/51] mfd: stmpe-i2c: treat a missing interrupt as no-irq
 mode

The I2C core reports a client without an interrupt as irq 0, but the
stmpe core only switches to its no-irq variant (which the STMPE801 has)
when the irq is negative. With 0 it goes on to request irq 0 and the
probe fails, so an STMPE801 whose INT pin is not connected could not be
used from DT. Pass -1 when the client has no interrupt.

Tested on a Sony Ericsson P1i (STMPE801 at 0x41, INT not connected).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/mfd/stmpe-i2c.c | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mfd/stmpe-i2c.c b/drivers/mfd/stmpe-i2c.c
index 4d55494a9..09837b1c6 100644
--- a/drivers/mfd/stmpe-i2c.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/stmpe-i2c.c
@@ -73,7 +73,8 @@ stmpe_i2c_probe(struct i2c_client *i2c, const struct i2c_device_id *id)
 	const struct of_device_id *of_id;
 
 	i2c_ci.data = (void *)id;
-	i2c_ci.irq = i2c->irq;
+	/* The I2C core reports "no interrupt" as 0; the stmpe core expects < 0. */
+	i2c_ci.irq = i2c->irq > 0 ? i2c->irq : -1;
 	i2c_ci.client = i2c;
 	i2c_ci.dev = &i2c->dev;
 
